fact WorkItem(id: "WI-663", description: some(value: "CARRIER-AGNOSTIC head readers (bucket 2 of the carrier-agnostic-heads umbrella): rule_head (mod.rs, the term-only head reader) PANICS on a value-fact head ('rule_head: head is not a Term carrier -- a value fact reached a term-only head reader (WI-348); read via rule_head_value / TermView instead'). This is the HARD-looking pressure that keeps rule heads as hash-consed terms, but it is NOT architectural: (a) LOWER the panic to a graceful LoadError -- loud-but-not-fatal (repo 'loud error over silent skip' principle) -- so a value head reaching an un-migrated reader is a clear load-time diagnostic, never a process abort; (b) MIGRATE the term-only head readers to carrier-agnostic rule_head_value / TermView where they only need functor + structure. Readers to audit/migrate (consumers of rules_by_functor results + others): stored_lhs_functor (simp_rewrite.rs, rule_head), open_equation (simp_rewrite.rs, rule_head), rule_is_general_eq_clause (typing.rs, WI-650, rule_head + get_term), the resolve_sort_alias / build_sort_alias_index scans (fact_head_term -- currently SKIP value heads, WI-659), and any get_term-on-head path. IMPORTANT ordering: bucket-1 (replace the by-subfield rules_by_functor scans with typed indexes: WI-656/659/660/...) REMOVES several of these readers outright -- do this migration AFTER those land so the surface is minimal. WI-394 (term-only walk_type -> carrier-agnostic walk_view) is the exact template. Acceptance: the rule_head-family readers either handle value heads via TermView or emit a LoadError (no panic) on a genuine value-head-in-term-only context; full suite green; a synthetic value-fact head reaching a migrated reader resolves (or LoadErrors) instead of panicking. Part of the carrier-agnostic-heads umbrella; see WI-348 (introduced the trip-wire), WI-394 (template)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-10T03:51:30Z"))

